Held user shader time: a window can give the shaders it paints one time.

`Window::hold_user_shader_time(Some(t))` stamps every user shader painted
while it's set, primitive, quad and region alike, with `t`. The renderer
gives that draw `t` and a zero `delta` in place of its clock, and the clock
goes on ticking underneath, so a draw outside the hold carries on where it
would have been. A primitive's clock is shared by every draw of one program
in a window, so the hold rides the draw, not the clock: the time goes in
`UserShader::pad`, alignment the GPU never reads (the bits, then 1 while
held), and a run of instances only batches while that matches. A region
carries it as a plain field. With nothing held the pad stays zero, every
run batches as before and every draw reads its clock, so nothing painted
changes. Covers blade and DirectX 11, the two backends that draw user
shaders.

rox uses it for `debug.canvas_clock`, which holds a canvas's shader items
on the moment its time routes are held on, so two builds' pixels compare.

Not a backport. A gpui bump carries this forward with b0 and e4.

